<p>The net surplus position of the banking system for January 1997 is likely to be around 14,000 million rupees. This surplus would be further augmented by foreign capital inflows and to the extent the first tranche of India Development Bonds, due for redemption on January 15, have been gifted to resident Indians.</p>
<p>In sum, next month should be fairly easy on the money markets and call money rates can be expected to stabilize to single digit levels. The price gains recorded by government bonds, particularly those with a residual maturity of less than five years, will see further consolidation in January. However, one eye needs to be kept on RBI's actions in the forex market. Lessons learnt from February 1996 would suggest that a possible weakening of the rupee in early 1997 and the central bank's efforts to stabilize it could put severe pressure on the money markets and assets should be liquidated at first signals of increased volatility in the exchange rate.</p>
